1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,600
FLUX.1-dev is trained near 1024², a grid of 64×64 tokens.

2
00:00:03,800 --> 00:00:07,000
At 4096² the grid has 16× the tokens; attention costs about 256× as much.

3
00:00:07,200 --> 00:00:09,700
Offsets between tokens reach 255, where training saw at most 63.

4
00:00:09,900 --> 00:00:12,700
Full attention then returns stripes or a flat field.

5
00:00:12,900 --> 00:00:16,200
Split one query's keys into a near set and a far set.

6
00:00:16,400 --> 00:00:20,000
Full attention is exactly a mix of both, weighted by the near mass m.

7
00:00:20,200 --> 00:00:23,700
Measured at 1024²: m is 0.90 for a local head, 0.22 for a global head.

8
00:00:23,900 --> 00:00:27,200
Local view: an exact 64×64 window, the text and 1,024 bridge tokens.

9
00:00:27,400 --> 00:00:30,400
Global view: one token per 4×4 block, the whole image as a 64×64 grid.

10
00:00:30,600 --> 00:00:33,600
Each head has its own weight π. Window and grid offsets stay within 63.

11
00:00:33,800 --> 00:00:36,900
FLUX.1-dev has 57 blocks of 24 heads: 1,368 heads in all.

12
00:00:37,100 --> 00:00:40,500
Each curve is one head's weight π on its window, over the noise level σ.

13
00:00:40,700 --> 00:00:43,900
AReA gives every head one switch: weight 0 above σ = 0.89, then 1.

14
00:00:44,100 --> 00:00:47,100
Take the share of attention within 16 tokens, after the first 8 steps.

15
00:00:47,300 --> 00:00:50,200
Which head it is explains 97.5% of its variance; the noise level, 2.5%.

16
00:00:50,400 --> 00:00:52,600
Reach is a property of the head, not of the step.

17
00:00:52,700 --> 00:00:55,800
At 4096², full attention is the failure, so it cannot serve as the reference.

18
00:00:56,000 --> 00:00:59,000
At 1024², a 64×64 grid, the model's own full attention is still valid.

19
00:00:59,200 --> 00:01:02,400
Shrink both views by r = 4: a 16×16 window and a 16×16 grid.

20
00:01:02,600 --> 00:01:05,600
The image-to-window ratio is that of 4096²: 64/16 = 256/64 = 4.

21
00:01:05,800 --> 00:01:08,000
r = 2 emulates 2048² the same way.

22
00:01:08,200 --> 00:01:10,500
Full attention and both views see the same queries, keys and values.

23
00:01:10,600 --> 00:01:14,100
Sum over prompts, steps, queries and channels, per block, head, noise level and ratio.

24
00:01:14,300 --> 00:01:17,300
The squared error J(π) of a weight π is a parabola.

25
00:01:17,500 --> 00:01:20,300
On [0, 1], J is minimized by π* = clip(B/A, 0, 1), in closed form.

26
00:01:20,500 --> 00:01:23,700
One emulation pass over 32 prompts at 1024² fills the reach table.

27
00:01:23,900 --> 00:01:26,500
Tables fitted on disjoint prompt halves agree: correlation 0.989 at r = 2.

28
00:01:26,700 --> 00:01:28,900
One reach table per model, for every target size.

29
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:31,900
The main configuration keeps HRDiT's released three-stage schedule.

30
00:01:32,100 --> 00:01:35,300
30 steps at 1024² with the model's own full attention set the layout.

31
00:01:35,500 --> 00:01:38,800
Decode, upsample, sharpen, re-encode, re-noise to σ = 0.81: the last 17 steps at 2048².

32
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:41,900
Re-noise to σ = 0.61: the last 10 steps at 4096².

33
00:01:42,100 --> 00:01:44,400
Each SCA stage reads the reach table at its ratio: 2, then 4.

34
00:01:44,700 --> 00:01:50,300
The final 4096² image, with a crop at native resolution.

